I dug deeper into the parser rules and lexer definitions of units.py to identify all the natural language and date-math capabilities it has that are currently missing from fluxcap.
Here is the comprehensive breakdown of the gaps:
1. Numeric Dates (US and ISO formatting)
fluxcap expects verbose English, but units.py natively parses raw slashes and dashes:
*   MM/DD/YYYY (12/31/2018)
*   MM/DD/YY (12/31/18)
*   MM/DD (12/31)
*   YYYY-MM-DD (2018-12-31)
2. Time Formatting and AM/PM logic
fluxcap supports basic hourspec ("3pm", "15:00"), but units.py has dedicated, robust lexical support for exact clock times and 12-hour math:
*   HH:MM and HH:MM:SS (Standard and international)
*   Explicit AM/PM suffixing: p_verbose_time_pm mathematically adds 12 hours to any parsed time.
3. Named Times
units.py has a concept of named_time:
*   "noon"
*   "midnight"
*   "eod" or "end of day"
4. Named Dates (Holidays)
units.py has a p_named_date rule that lets you anchor logic to specific holidays (and optionally specify the year):
*   "christmas"
*   "christmas eve"
*   "christmas 2024"
5. ISO Week Numbering
fluxcap knows how to evaluate "a week", but it doesn't understand the ISO 8601 week calendar. units.py natively supports week-number calculations:
*   "Week 12"
*   "Week 12 2018" or "Week 12 / 2018"
*   "Week 12 of 2018"
*   "first week of 2018" / "last week of 2018"
6. Quarter and Half Shorthands
While fluxcap supports the words "quarter" and "half", units.py explicitly supports their corporate shorthands combined with years:
*   "Q1" / "Q1 2018"
*   "H2" / "H2 2019"
7. Explicit "First/Last" of an Interval
fluxcap relies on its generic ordinal grammar ([ordinal] sequence of time_span, e.g., "1st month of the year"), whereas units.py has dedicated shortcut grammar specifically optimized for first/last:
*   "first of next month"
*   "last of this year"
*   "first day of next month"
